'''If''':

Note that $$\begin{align*}\measuredangle GBT+\measuredangle TRG&=\frac{m\widehat{TQ}}{2}+\measuredangle TRB+\measuredangle BRG\\
&=\frac{m\widehat{TQ}+m\widehat{DQ}+m\widehat{CB}+m\widehat{BT}}{2}.\\
\end{align*}$$
Thus, $BTRG$ is cyclic.

Also, note that $GSCP$ is cyclic because $$\begin{align*}\measuredangle CSG+\measuredangle GPC&=\measuredangle CBA+\measuredangle APC\\
&=180^\circ\text{ or }0^\circ,
\end{align*}$$ depending on the configuration.

Next, we have $T, G, C$ are collinear since $$\measuredangle GTR=\measuredangle GBR=\frac{m\widehat{DQ}}{2}=\frac{m\widehat{QC}}{2}=\measuredangle CTQ.$$

Therefore, $$\begin{align*}\measuredangle RQP+\measuredangle PSR&=\frac{m\widehat{PBT}}{2}+\measuredangle PCG\\
&=\frac{m\widehat{PBT}+m\widehat{TDP}}{2}\\
&=180^\circ
\end{align*},$$ so $PQRS$ is cyclic.

'''Only If''':
These steps can be reversed.

By the [[Law_of_Sines|sine law]] on triangle $AB'P$,
$$\frac{AB'}{\sin \angle APB'} = \frac{AP}{\sin \angle AB'P},$$
so
$$AB' = AP \cdot \frac{\sin \angle APB'}{\sin \angle AB'P}.$$

<asy>
import graph;
import geometry;

